56
00:03:37,230 --> 00:03:41,930
with immunocompromised
issues, including HIV. Now

57
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:50,180
we will discuss pneumonia
in community setting.

58
00:03:50,340 --> 00:03:52,920
Firstly, I will
describe bacterial

59
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:56,020
pneumonia and later
the interstitial form.

60
00:03:56,960 --> 00:04:01,010
By definition, bacterial
pneumonia represents

61
00:04:01,010 --> 00:04:03,200
exudative inflammation
of the small

62
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:07,420
caliber airways extending
from lung parenchyma,

63
00:04:07,450 --> 00:04:10,280
which of course is
caused by a bacteria.

64
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:13,260
This inflammation
determines the

65
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:15,400
so-called
consolidation of lung.

66
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:20,060
Predisposing conditions
are represented by

67
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:24,180
extremes of age, elderly
or young children.

68
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:30,220
Bacterial pneumonia is
seen in higher numbers in

69
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:33,140
patients with congestive
heart failure, diabetes,

70
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:36,940
COPD, immune deficiencies.

71
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:41,960
Bacterial pneumonia is
also encountered with

72
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:45,960
higher incidence in
patients with absent splenic

73
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:48,700
function which are at
increased risk of infections

74
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:52,600
with encapsulated bacteria
such as pneumococcus.

75
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:55,720
Anyway, these patients
would present with

76
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:59,110
an abrupt onset of
high fever, chills and

77
00:04:59,110 --> 00:05:02,500
cough producing a rusty
mucopurulin sputum.

78
00:05:03,260 --> 00:05:06,940
Rarely, the patients might
present with hemoptysis.

79
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:11,040
if the inflammation
extends from the lungs

80
00:05:11,040 --> 00:05:14,280
to the pleura the
patients will develop pain

81
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:17,720
on inspiration and
pleural friction rub

82
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:20,740
might be identified on
physical examination

83
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:25,240
the fundamental basis
for bacterial pneumonia

84
00:05:25,240 --> 00:05:28,300
therapy is represented
of course by antibiotics

85
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:33,960
if the bacteria is identified
targeted antibiotic

86
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:37,240
therapy is efficient
in most cases and the

87
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:41,260
patient will become afebrile
in less than 2-3 days.

88
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:44,920
However, if the
antibiotics are given

89
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:47,620
empirically before the
identification of the

90
00:05:47,620 --> 00:05:51,380
etiological agent or in
an appropriate dosage,

91
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:54,040
the outcome might not
be always optimal.

92
00:06:02,220 --> 00:06:04,140
Now let's talk a
little bit about some

93
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:07,160
elements of bacterial
pneumonia morphology.

94
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:10,700
This condition is
seen in two forms,

95
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:13,440
bronchopneumonia
and ***** pneumonia.

96
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:18,020
bronchopneumonia is
mostly encountered at the

97
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:21,520
extremes of age and is the
extension of an initial

98
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:25,940
bronchitis or bronchiolitis
into alveoli with

99
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:30,320
formation of a patchy
consolidation this patchy

100
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:33,380
consolidation may involve
more than one lobe

101
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:38,700
the most common causes
of bronchopneumonia are

102
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:42,700
streptococcus Pneumonia,
Staphylococcus aureus,

103
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:46,860
Haemophilus influenzae,
and Klebsiella Pneumonia.

104
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:52,180
On the other hand, *****
Pneumonia represents the

105
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:56,080
consolidation of a large
portion of lobe or entire

106
00:06:56,080 --> 00:07:01,980
lobe caused by intra-alveolar
exudate determined by

107
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:06,180
Streptococcus Pneumonia,
Legionella or Klebsiella.

108
00:07:12,090 --> 00:07:15,130
Natural history of
lower pneumonia.

109
00:07:15,530 --> 00:07:18,610
During the natural
history or evolution

110
00:07:18,610 --> 00:07:21,130
of lower pneumonia
there are four stages.

111
00:07:22,030 --> 00:07:24,690
Initially during
the first two days

112
00:07:24,690 --> 00:07:27,730
the lungs are heavy,
boggy and red.

113
00:07:28,290 --> 00:07:30,690
There is consolidation of lung

114
00:07:30,690 --> 00:07:33,190
parenchyma caused by
intra-alveolar edema

115
00:07:33,690 --> 00:07:37,290
containing few neutrophils
and some bacteria.

116
00:07:37,830 --> 00:07:41,450
In addition there is
vascular engorgement.

117
00:07:42,370 --> 00:07:47,230
The next stage is
red hepatization and

118
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:49,710
this is described
during days 3 and 4.

119
00:07:51,090 --> 00:07:56,090
At this point, there is
red-brown consolidation of

120
00:07:56,090 --> 00:08:00,950
the lung with a liver-like
consistency and a massive

121
00:08:00,950 --> 00:08:04,630
confluent exudation as
neutrophils, red blood

122
00:08:04,630 --> 00:08:08,850
cells, bacteria, and fibrin
fill the alveolar spaces.

123
00:08:10,530 --> 00:08:13,750
Red hepatization is followed by

124
00:08:13,750 --> 00:08:17,790
gray hepatization
at days 5 to 7.

125
00:08:17,930 --> 00:08:21,630
This is characterized
microscopically by the

126
00:08:21,630 --> 00:08:25,970
presence of an alveolar
exudate made of neutrophils,

127
00:08:26,170 --> 00:08:28,190
disintegrated red
blood cells, and

128
00:08:28,190 --> 00:08:32,550
fibrin, resulting in
a uniformly grayish

129
00:08:32,550 --> 00:08:34,930
-brown color of the
lung parenchyma.

130
00:08:36,070 --> 00:08:41,670
The final stage of resolution
is seen after 8 days.

131
00:08:41,890 --> 00:08:43,710
During this stage,

132
00:08:43,870 --> 00:08:46,570
the alveolar exudate is broken

133
00:08:46,570 --> 00:08:49,430
down by infiltrated
macrophages.

134
00:08:50,190 --> 00:08:54,370
A semi-fluid debris is
produced and subsequently

135
00:08:54,370 --> 00:08:57,970
is resorbed and ingested
by these macrophages.

136
00:08:59,930 --> 00:09:03,850
Part of it is expectorated
as a rusty mucopurulent

137
00:09:03,850 --> 00:09:08,210
sputum, or organized by
fibroblasts growing into it.

138
00:09:11,850 --> 00:09:14,390
On this slide, we
have two images

139
00:09:14,390 --> 00:09:17,010
representing the red
hepatization stage.

140
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:20,810
On the left side image,
the dissected lung

141
00:09:20,810 --> 00:09:23,710
displays a red, boggy,
congestive aspect.

142
00:09:24,250 --> 00:09:28,990
The right side microscopic
image reveals several

143
00:09:28,990 --> 00:09:32,930
alveoli which are filled
with numerous neutrophils.

144
00:09:33,290 --> 00:09:36,510
The septal capillaries
are congested.

145
00:09:36,670 --> 00:09:37,870
However,

146
00:09:38,850 --> 00:09:41,370
fibrin strands are not
seen at this stage.

147
00:09:43,870 --> 00:09:46,990
The images on this
slide represent

148
00:09:46,990 --> 00:09:49,070
the gray hepatization stage.

149
00:09:49,250 --> 00:09:53,770
The first photo from left
is remarkable for a right

150
00:09:53,770 --> 00:09:57,950
lower lobe, which is uniformly
gray and consolidated.

151
00:09:58,460 --> 00:10:01,510
While in the middle
image, both the

152
00:10:01,510 --> 00:10:03,530
middle and the lower
lobes of the right

153
00:10:03,530 --> 00:10:06,750
lung appear gray,
solid and airless.

154
00:10:07,610 --> 00:10:12,910
The right side microscopic
image represents a hematoxylin

155
00:10:12,910 --> 00:10:16,590
eosin stain tissue section
taken from a case of

156
00:10:16,590 --> 00:10:21,800
bacterial pneumonia in gray
hepatization stage Apart

157
00:10:21,810 --> 00:10:25,470
from an abundant exudate
made of mostly neutrophils,

158
00:10:25,610 --> 00:10:28,890
we can distinguish
numerous fibrin strands

159
00:10:32,610 --> 00:10:35,750
bronchopneumonia morphology
as previously mentioned

160
00:10:35,750 --> 00:10:38,430
bronchopneumonia is
characterized by the presence

161
00:10:38,430 --> 00:10:42,070
of patchy consolidated
areas which appear firmer

162
00:10:42,070 --> 00:10:46,370
than surrounding lung the
superlative exudate is

163
00:10:46,370 --> 00:10:49,330
seen in one lobe or it may
extend in several lobes

164
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:53,950
this exudate at the microscopic
level would fill the

165
00:10:53,950 --> 00:10:57,210
bronchi the bronchioles
and the adjacent alveoli.

166
00:10:57,630 --> 00:11:01,210
The left side microscopic
image shows the

167
00:11:01,210 --> 00:11:04,400
cut surface of a lung
with bronchopneumonia.

168
00:11:05,410 --> 00:11:08,830
As you can see, this
is characterized by

169
00:11:08,830 --> 00:11:11,090
the presence of slightly
elevated granular

170
00:11:11,090 --> 00:11:13,780
areas of tiny yellow
consolidation.

171
00:11:14,910 --> 00:11:19,850
The right side
microscopic image shows

172
00:11:19,950 --> 00:11:23,610
the presence of a
neutrophil-rich infiltrate,

173
00:11:23,610 --> 00:11:27,510
which fills all the
smaller airways.

174
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:33,410
Now I would like to mention
that in these patients with

175
00:11:33,410 --> 00:11:36,610
bronchopneumonia, sometimes
we see massive tissue

176
00:11:36,610 --> 00:11:40,970
destruction and necrosis and
abscess formation. Some of

177
00:11:40,970 --> 00:11:45,370
the patients will develop
empyema, which suggests the

178
00:11:45,370 --> 00:11:48,130
spread of inflammation and
destruction to the pleura.

179
00:11:48,130 --> 00:11:53,030
There is also organization
of exudate to a solid tissue

180
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:57,750
and in rare cases we can
see bacteremia with the

181
00:11:57,750 --> 00:12:02,190
dissemination to heart valves,
pericardium, brain, kidney,

182
00:12:02,470 --> 00:12:04,050
spleen or joints.

183
00:12:08,110 --> 00:12:10,510
Interstitial or
atypical pneumonia.

184
00:12:10,690 --> 00:12:13,550
This condition is
caused by various

185
00:12:13,550 --> 00:12:16,470
types of bacteria
such as mycoplasma,

186
00:12:16,730 --> 00:12:17,730
chlamydia,

187
00:12:18,010 --> 00:12:20,870
legionella, and
coxiella, or it might

188
00:12:20,870 --> 00:12:24,770
be produced by viruses
such as RCV, CMV,

189
00:12:25,030 --> 00:12:27,270
influenza, and adenoviruses.

190
00:12:27,470 --> 00:12:30,230
This pathological
entity is characterized

191
00:12:30,230 --> 00:12:32,750
by diffuse patch
inflammation of

192
00:12:32,750 --> 00:12:35,330
the interstitium
and alveolar walls.

193
00:12:35,490 --> 00:12:38,310
On chest X-ray,
there are bilateral

194
00:12:38,310 --> 00:12:40,970
multifocal opacities, but these

195
00:12:40,970 --> 00:12:43,290
conditions follow a
rather benign course,

196
00:12:43,430 --> 00:12:46,750
hence the name
walking pneumonia.

197
00:12:47,070 --> 00:12:50,570
By the way, this
type of pneumonia is

198
00:12:50,570 --> 00:12:52,870
labeled atypical
as it characterized

199
00:12:52,870 --> 00:12:56,210
by moderate amount
of sputum. However,

200
00:12:56,550 --> 00:12:59,510
the patients do not
show any physical signs

201
00:12:59,510 --> 00:13:03,050
of consolidation.
No alveolar exudate

202
00:13:03,050 --> 00:13:06,810
is seen histologically
in these patients.

203
00:13:08,490 --> 00:13:11,610
Let's discuss now
viral pneumonia.

204
00:13:12,410 --> 00:13:16,790
This viral pneumonia is
caused by various viral

205
00:13:16,790 --> 00:13:20,070
agents that have a
tropism, allowing them

206
00:13:20,070 --> 00:13:23,490
to attach and enter
respiratory lining cells.

207
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:27,710
Inside these cells, the
viruses replicate and

208
00:13:27,710 --> 00:13:30,990
cause cellular death and
secondary inflammation.

209
00:13:31,470 --> 00:13:33,370
In some patients,

210
00:13:33,930 --> 00:13:36,950
local pulmonary
defense mechanisms such

211
00:13:36,950 --> 00:13:39,970
as mucociliary
clearance are defective.

212
00:13:40,310 --> 00:13:43,710
As a result of this,
superimposed bacterial

213
00:13:43,710 --> 00:13:46,530
infections may occur.
However, in some

214
00:13:46,530 --> 00:13:50,790
patients, these bacterial
infections are much

215
00:13:50,790 --> 00:13:53,650
more severe than the
viral infection per se.

216
00:13:54,230 --> 00:13:55,430
Anyway,

217
00:13:55,810 --> 00:13:58,730
viral pneumonia is
seen frequently in

218
00:13:58,730 --> 00:14:01,330
newborns and elderly,
as well as in

219
00:14:01,330 --> 00:14:03,950
those with malnutrition
and alcoholism.

220
00:14:09,180 --> 00:14:10,980
Viral pneumonia morphology

221
00:14:12,140 --> 00:14:15,600
All viral agents cause
similar morphological

222
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:18,280
changes consistent with
this type of pneumonia.

223
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:21,860
Initially, there is an
upper respiratory infection,

224
00:14:21,860 --> 00:14:24,700
characterized by
mucosal hyperemia and

225
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:28,220
swelling, as well as
increased mucus production.

226
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:30,360
In this setting,

227
00:14:30,840 --> 00:14:33,960
a hyperemic swollen
mucosa and a viscous

228
00:14:33,960 --> 00:14:38,600
exudate leads to a secondary
superlative bacterial

229
00:14:38,600 --> 00:14:41,560
infection, caused by
blockage of the nasal

230
00:14:41,560 --> 00:14:45,740
channels, sinuses, and
or eustachian tubes.

231
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:50,720
Eventually, the inflammatory
infectious process extends

232
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:54,300
towards the lung parenchia,
which might show a patchy

233
00:14:54,300 --> 00:14:58,480
or a lobular aspect
unilaterally or on both lungs.

234
00:14:59,500 --> 00:15:02,660
The affected lung is congested,

235
00:15:02,660 --> 00:15:05,360
displaying a red-blue color.

236
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:08,460
Very rarely, some
patients might

237
00:15:08,460 --> 00:15:11,260
develop pleuritis or
pleural effusions.

238
00:15:11,980 --> 00:15:14,960
The histological
pattern reflects

239
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:16,660
the severity of the disease.

240
00:15:17,320 --> 00:15:18,130
In

241
00:15:18,140 --> 00:15:21,460
general, we see in these
cases an interstitial

242
00:15:21,460 --> 00:15:25,160
inflammatory reaction in
the alveolar walls and

243
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:28,460
interstitium, but as
mentioned before, there is no

244
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:31,860
inflammatory exudate in
the alveoli unless the

245
00:15:31,860 --> 00:15:35,760
patient has developed a
bacterial supra-infection.

246
00:15:36,250 --> 00:15:39,170
The image seen in
this slide represents

247
00:15:39,180 --> 00:15:42,460
a microscopic photo
of a section of lung

248
00:15:42,460 --> 00:15:45,780
parenchyma stayed with
hematoxylin eosin.

249
00:15:45,940 --> 00:15:48,680
There is a significant
inflammatory

250
00:15:48,680 --> 00:15:51,260
infiltrate in the
walls of the alveoli.

251
00:15:51,260 --> 00:15:55,520
The alveolar receptors
widen and contain

252
00:15:55,520 --> 00:15:58,400
inflammatory mononuclear
cells, lymphocytes.

253
00:15:59,080 --> 00:16:02,440
In patients that develop
acute respiratory

254
00:16:02,440 --> 00:16:06,520
distress syndrome, such
as this case, we can

255
00:16:06,520 --> 00:16:09,860
identify hyaline membranes
in the alveolar walls.

256
00:16:10,420 --> 00:16:13,520
However, if the
patient survives,

257
00:16:14,140 --> 00:16:16,400
as they do in
majority of cases,

258
00:16:16,640 --> 00:16:19,360
the healing process
leads to reconstitution

259
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:21,720
of the normal lung structure.

260
00:16:25,700 --> 00:16:28,160
Histological evaluation
of lung parenchyma

261
00:16:28,160 --> 00:16:30,740
may reveal in some cases
important information

262
00:16:30,740 --> 00:16:32,980
regarding the actual
etiological agent.

263
00:16:33,480 --> 00:16:36,540
The left side,
microphotograph, was taken

264
00:16:36,540 --> 00:16:38,880
from a case of
adenoviral pneumonia.

265
00:16:39,840 --> 00:16:43,640
The tissue evaluation
reveals cellular debris

266
00:16:43,640 --> 00:16:46,980
and individual epithelial
cells with dark viral

267
00:16:46,980 --> 00:16:50,780
inclusions suggestive
of adenovirus infection.

268
00:16:51,180 --> 00:16:55,540
The right side, microphotograph,
represents a section

269
00:16:55,540 --> 00:16:59,180
of lung parenchyma from a
patient with CMV pneumonia.

270
00:16:59,880 --> 00:17:02,040
As you might see in this image,

271
00:17:02,340 --> 00:17:05,600
histological evaluation
reveals enlarged cells,

272
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:09,920
cytomegaly, with
basophilic cytoplasmic

273
00:17:09,920 --> 00:17:13,600
inclusions, as well as
intranuclear inclusions.

274
00:17:13,600 --> 00:17:18,460
All of these are diagnostic
for CMV pneumonia.

275
00:17:19,790 --> 00:17:23,020
Viral pneumonia caused
by influenza type A.

276
00:17:23,580 --> 00:17:26,600
Influenza type A
represents a major cause

277
00:17:26,600 --> 00:17:29,660
of pandemics or
epidemics worldwide.

278
00:17:29,880 --> 00:17:32,540
Its genome encodes
several proteins

279
00:17:32,540 --> 00:17:35,560
but the most important
are the hemagglutinin

280
00:17:35,560 --> 00:17:37,700
and the neuroaminidase
proteins.

281
00:17:38,680 --> 00:17:42,360
Both are embedded in
a lipid bilayer which

282
00:17:42,360 --> 00:17:44,980
constitutes the
influenza virus envelope.

283
00:17:45,980 --> 00:17:48,560
Hemagglutinin serves
to attach the virus

284
00:17:48,560 --> 00:17:52,140
to its cellular target
while neuroaminidase

285
00:17:52,140 --> 00:17:54,660
promote the release of
newly formed virions

286
00:17:54,660 --> 00:17:57,880
that are budding
from infected cells.

287
00:17:59,320 --> 00:18:02,580
Also the infected
cells release a

288
00:18:02,580 --> 00:18:05,820
variety of inflammatory
mediators including

289
00:18:05,820 --> 00:18:08,520
several chemokines
and cytokines.

290
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:10,480
In addition,

291
00:18:10,900 --> 00:18:12,920
cellular destruction
activates local

292
00:18:12,920 --> 00:18:15,820
inflammatory cells
including some

293
00:18:15,820 --> 00:18:19,640
macrophages, exacerbating
the inflammation.

294
00:18:20,880 --> 00:18:22,800
Very important in the

295
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:25,020
pathogenesis of
influenza type A

296
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:29,420
is the phenomenon of
antigenic drift, which

297
00:18:29,420 --> 00:18:32,220
represents a natural
mutation over time

298
00:18:32,760 --> 00:18:37,440
of known strains of
influenza A, which might lead

299
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:40,760
to loss of immunity or
in a vaccine mismatch.

300
00:18:42,380 --> 00:18:47,400
also influenza type A might
present with an antigenic

301
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:53,400
shift in this case an influenza
type A strain combines

302
00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:58,200
with a different strain
of the same virus or with

303
00:18:58,200 --> 00:19:02,940
strains of different type
of viruses resulting in a

304
00:19:02,940 --> 00:19:07,890
viral agent with a mixture
of surface antigens basically

305
00:19:08,700 --> 00:19:12,220
the hemagglutinin and the
neuroaminidase are replaced

306
00:19:12,220 --> 00:19:17,160
through a combination of
RNA segments with other

307
00:19:17,160 --> 00:19:22,440
viruses or with different
strains of influenza type A.

308
00:19:23,990 --> 00:19:28,440
Influenza type B and
C do not present with

309
00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:31,860
antigenic drift and
shift and are mostly

310
00:19:31,860 --> 00:19:34,980
important for the
pediatric population.

311
00:19:39,970 --> 00:19:43,530
Influenza type A morphology.
In this disease, the

312
00:19:43,530 --> 00:19:46,730
lungs would appear with
significant mucosal hyperemia.

313
00:19:46,810 --> 00:19:50,390
The sub-mucosa shows a marked
inflammatory infiltrate

314
00:19:50,390 --> 00:19:53,770
with lymphocytes,
monocytes, and plasma cells.

315
00:19:53,890 --> 00:19:56,570
The patient presents
with overproduction

316
00:19:56,570 --> 00:19:59,190
of mucus, and
some would develop

317
00:19:59,190 --> 00:20:01,910
bacterial
superinfection and ARDS.

318
00:20:02,290 --> 00:20:05,950
The left side image represents
a microscopic photon

319
00:20:05,950 --> 00:20:10,230
taken from a lung dissected
in a sagittal plane, and

320
00:20:10,230 --> 00:20:14,070
the patient in this case
died of influenza pneumonia.

321
00:20:14,330 --> 00:20:17,150
The general
impression, the general

322
00:20:17,150 --> 00:20:20,370
pattern, is congestion
and heaviness.

323
00:20:21,880 --> 00:20:24,610
Microscopically, as you
can see in the right

324
00:20:24,610 --> 00:20:27,510
side image, the lung
parenchyma shows alveolar

325
00:20:27,510 --> 00:20:31,750
walls, which are distended,
widened, and thickened

326
00:20:31,750 --> 00:20:34,710
by an inflammatory
infiltrate, and there is

327
00:20:34,710 --> 00:20:37,930
a significant formation
of hyaline membranes.

328
00:20:38,410 --> 00:20:40,810
Highly membranes
formation indicates

329
00:20:40,810 --> 00:20:43,390
significant damage
to alveolar walls.

330
00:20:45,570 --> 00:20:47,570
Interstitial atypical pneumonia

331
00:20:47,570 --> 00:20:49,570
caused by mycoplasma pneumonia.

332
00:20:49,740 --> 00:20:52,690
This is relatively common
in children and young

333
00:20:52,690 --> 00:20:56,390
adults and might be seen
sporadically in individual

334
00:20:56,390 --> 00:21:00,490
cases as well as in epidemics
in close communities.

335
00:21:01,190 --> 00:21:04,450
Mycoplasma pneumonia is
a very small bacteria

336
00:21:04,450 --> 00:21:09,070
that has adherence proteins
that facilitates its

337
00:21:09,070 --> 00:21:12,710
attachment to the
respiratory tract epithelium.

338
00:21:12,950 --> 00:21:17,310
Once attached, it produces
hydrogen peroxide,

339
00:21:17,310 --> 00:21:20,550
causing injury to the
respiratory epithelium.

340
00:21:20,950 --> 00:21:24,470
There is also activation of the

341
00:21:24,470 --> 00:21:26,710
inflammatory factors
and pathways.

342
00:21:27,170 --> 00:21:28,610
Remarkably,

343
00:21:28,750 --> 00:21:33,190
antibodies such as
IgM and IgG produced

344
00:21:33,190 --> 00:21:37,610
against mycoplasma could
act as autoantibodies

345
00:21:37,610 --> 00:21:40,670
cross-reacting with
human brain cells and

346
00:21:40,670 --> 00:21:41,990
red blood cells.

347
00:21:42,530 --> 00:21:45,530
The final result of red blood

348
00:21:45,530 --> 00:21:47,690
cell destruction is hemolysis.

349
00:21:48,790 --> 00:21:51,110
This patient would present with

350
00:21:51,110 --> 00:21:53,310
nonspecific signs and symptoms.

351
00:21:54,390 --> 00:21:58,690
Also in numerous cases the
patients are asymptomatic.

352
00:22:00,050 --> 00:22:03,770
The symptoms are typically
more significant than

353
00:22:03,770 --> 00:22:09,530
objective findings and the
disease onset is gradual and

354
00:22:09,530 --> 00:22:13,250
patient can initially complain
of headaches malaise and

355
00:22:13,250 --> 00:22:16,750
low-grade fever there are
no chills in these patients

356
00:22:17,970 --> 00:22:23,570
usually the patients would
present with the dry cough

357
00:22:23,570 --> 00:22:27,490
which lasts for a while
as the epithelial ciliary

358
00:22:27,490 --> 00:22:31,910
movement that promotes
expectoration is inhibited by this

359
00:22:31,910 --> 00:22:38,350
bacteria on physical
examination there are diffuse

360
00:22:38,350 --> 00:22:43,830
crackles on both lung fields
most cases of pneumonia

361
00:22:43,830 --> 00:22:50,430
caused by mycoplasma are self
-limited but 10 to 20 percent

362
00:22:50,430 --> 00:22:54,650
of these patients will
develop pleural effusions.

363
00:22:55,570 --> 00:22:58,430
Also, some patients
will develop

364
00:22:58,670 --> 00:23:02,650
hemolysis, which
occurs, as I said,

365
00:23:03,390 --> 00:23:09,530
due to IgM antibodies, which
induce a cold agglutinin

366
00:23:09,530 --> 00:23:13,230
reaction associated with
red blood cell destruction.

367
00:23:13,770 --> 00:23:20,390
Other patients might
develop ITP, idiopathic

368
00:23:20,390 --> 00:23:24,270
thrombocytopenic purpura,
acute hepatitis, arthritis,

369
00:23:24,430 --> 00:23:26,490
and transverse mellitus.

370
00:23:27,070 --> 00:23:29,950
Some patients
might also present

371
00:23:29,950 --> 00:23:32,810
with GI symptoms
and heart disease.

372
00:23:37,190 --> 00:23:40,170
Now, on this slide, I
have mentioned the most

373
00:23:40,170 --> 00:23:43,390
important elements of morphology
in pneumonia determined

374
00:23:43,390 --> 00:23:47,050
by mycoplasma. As
mentioned, in this setting,

375
00:23:47,190 --> 00:23:50,090
respiratory epithelial
injury occurs after

376
00:23:50,090 --> 00:23:53,210
attachment of mycoplasma
to respiratory mucosa.

377
00:23:53,560 --> 00:23:56,310
The lesions may be
patchy or may involve

378
00:23:56,310 --> 00:23:59,310
whole lobes, bilaterally
or unilaterally.

379
00:23:59,740 --> 00:24:04,330
Please note that as
mycoplasma secretes peroxide,

380
00:24:04,330 --> 00:24:06,600
there might be
ulceration of the mucosa.

381
00:24:06,670 --> 00:24:10,550
The microphotograph
included here represents a

382
00:24:10,550 --> 00:24:13,930
hematoxylinal neosin-stained
lung parenchyma section

383
00:24:13,930 --> 00:24:18,410
from a case of pneumonia
determined by mycoplasma.

384
00:24:18,410 --> 00:24:21,530
There is a significant
interstitial inflammatory

385
00:24:21,530 --> 00:24:25,750
reaction localized within
the walls of the alveoli, and

386
00:24:25,750 --> 00:24:29,770
as a result, the alveolar
septae appear widened.

387
00:24:29,910 --> 00:24:33,470
The inflammatory infiltrate
is made mostly of

388
00:24:33,470 --> 00:24:35,970
lymphocytes, histiocytes,
and plasma cells.

389
00:24:36,670 --> 00:24:40,950
As you can see here, we
could identify an intra

390
00:24:40,950 --> 00:24:44,310
-alveolar proteinaceous
material in the alveoli.

391
00:24:48,700 --> 00:24:50,710
Now, I would like
to discuss the

392
00:24:50,710 --> 00:24:52,810
health care or
nosocomial pneumonia.

393
00:24:52,990 --> 00:24:56,590
This is a distinct entity
which is defined as the

394
00:24:56,590 --> 00:24:59,610
pulmonary infection associated
with hospitalization of

395
00:24:59,610 --> 00:25:02,850
at least two days in patients
with serious underlying

396
00:25:02,850 --> 00:25:06,290
conditions, including
immunosuppression and cancer.

397
00:25:06,290 --> 00:25:09,310
It is seen in those
receiving IV antibiotic

398
00:25:09,310 --> 00:25:12,290
therapy, in patients
with IV catheters,

399
00:25:12,290 --> 00:25:14,730
and in those on
mechanical ventilation.

400
00:25:15,120 --> 00:25:18,410
The mortality is
significantly higher than in

401
00:25:18,410 --> 00:25:20,590
the patients with community
-acquired pneumonia.

402
00:25:20,910 --> 00:25:24,690
The most important etiological
agents are the gram

403
00:25:24,690 --> 00:25:28,010
-positive staphylococcus
aureus and the gram-negative

404
00:25:28,010 --> 00:25:31,930
rhodes such as pseudomonas
and enterobacteria C.

405
00:25:34,310 --> 00:25:37,430
First of all, I would
like to mention that as

406
00:25:37,430 --> 00:25:40,330
methicillin has been used
all around the world since

407
00:25:40,330 --> 00:25:44,290
the 1960s and has been
inappropriately administered,

408
00:25:44,290 --> 00:25:46,650
some patients would
develop pulmonary

409
00:25:46,650 --> 00:25:50,430
infections with methicillin
-resistant staph aureus.

410
00:25:50,650 --> 00:25:54,830
These patients oftentimes
will present with

411
00:25:54,830 --> 00:25:57,300
a clinical picture of
a bronchopneumonia.

412
00:25:58,650 --> 00:26:02,250
Patients that develop
pseudomonas aeruginosa

413
00:26:02,250 --> 00:26:07,870
infections are those
with cystic fibrosis,

414
00:26:07,870 --> 00:26:10,530
those with emphysema,
and in patients

415
00:26:10,530 --> 00:26:14,130
that they are hospitalized
for long term.

416
00:26:14,910 --> 00:26:20,210
The macroscopic image
on the left shows

417
00:26:20,210 --> 00:26:23,090
a dissected lung from
a patient that died

418
00:26:23,090 --> 00:26:27,810
from a Pseudomonas
aeruginosa pneumonia.

419
00:26:28,030 --> 00:26:31,590
The area affected by
Pseudomonas infection

420
00:26:31,590 --> 00:26:34,930
is indicated by an erythematous

421
00:26:34,930 --> 00:26:38,710
patch which is
surrounded by a tan rim.

422
00:26:39,150 --> 00:26:42,730
In these patients a subsequent

423
00:26:42,730 --> 00:26:44,430
cavitation might develop.

424
00:26:45,270 --> 00:26:49,910
Also at the level of this
cavitation an abscess

425
00:26:49,910 --> 00:26:55,160
might fall if the patient
is not well treated.

426
00:26:55,630 --> 00:26:59,530
Histologically, there
is massive inflammatory

427
00:26:59,530 --> 00:27:02,450
infiltration with neutrophils
in the lung parenchyma

428
00:27:02,450 --> 00:27:06,230
and alveoli consistent
with bronchopneumonia.

429
00:27:06,510 --> 00:27:09,630
On the right side,
microphotograph, you may

430
00:27:09,630 --> 00:27:14,050
see a neutrophilic
infiltrate as a basophilic

431
00:27:14,050 --> 00:27:18,390
blue areas in close
proximity with a large

432
00:27:18,390 --> 00:27:21,270
blood vessel. All of
these are characteristic

433
00:27:21,270 --> 00:27:24,710
for a lung infection
with Pseudomonas.

434
00:27:25,710 --> 00:27:29,790
Aspiration pneumonia. It
is described in debilitated

435
00:27:29,790 --> 00:27:32,710
patients or in those who
aspirate gastric contents

436
00:27:32,710 --> 00:27:36,170
either while unconscious or
during repeated vomiting.

437
00:27:36,470 --> 00:27:39,930
Apart from an altered
mental status with decreased

438
00:27:39,930 --> 00:27:43,830
cough reflexes or glottic
closure, the patients have

439
00:27:43,830 --> 00:27:48,190
dysphagia or they have a
nasogastric tube placed.

440
00:27:48,230 --> 00:27:52,450
It is also seen in bedridden
patients with stroke.

441
00:27:52,710 --> 00:27:55,090
The signs and symptoms develop

442
00:27:55,090 --> 00:27:57,370
several days after aspiration.

443
00:27:58,870 --> 00:28:02,970
This entity is partly
chemical due to the irritating

444
00:28:02,970 --> 00:28:06,970
effects of gastric acid
which is aspirated. The

445
00:28:06,970 --> 00:28:10,530
gastric acid induces an
acid-mediated inflammation

446
00:28:10,530 --> 00:28:15,670
which is called aspiration
chemical pneumonitis.

447
00:28:15,790 --> 00:28:17,210
However,

448
00:28:17,350 --> 00:28:21,630
aspiration pneumonia is
partly bacterial as more

449
00:28:21,630 --> 00:28:25,190
than one organisms are
recovered on culture,

450
00:28:25,710 --> 00:28:28,720
aerobes being more
common than anaerobes.

451
00:28:30,910 --> 00:28:35,970
Morphological evaluation in
these cases will reveal a

452
00:28:35,970 --> 00:28:40,210
necrotizing inflammation which
oftentimes has a fulminant

453
00:28:40,210 --> 00:28:43,210
clinical course and is a
frequent cause of death.

454
00:28:43,650 --> 00:28:46,430
If the patient survives, lung

455
00:28:46,430 --> 00:28:48,710
abscess is a common
complication.

456
00:28:49,410 --> 00:28:54,230
As represented on the
right side microphotograph,

457
00:28:54,710 --> 00:28:57,610
histological evaluation
of the lung parenchyma

458
00:28:57,610 --> 00:29:02,530
would show massive
destruction with a significant

459
00:29:02,530 --> 00:29:06,190
inflammatory infiltrate
made of neutrophils,

460
00:29:06,190 --> 00:29:08,250
eosinophils and lymphocytes.

461
00:29:10,310 --> 00:29:13,990
Lung abscess pathology.
By definition, the

462
00:29:13,990 --> 00:29:16,610
lung abscess represents
a collection of pus

463
00:29:16,610 --> 00:29:19,890
within lung parenchyma.
It is caused either

464
00:29:19,890 --> 00:29:23,890
by aspiration of
oropharyngeal contents or

465
00:29:23,890 --> 00:29:26,490
it might be caused by
bronchial obstruction.

466
00:29:26,710 --> 00:29:29,290
And any bacterial pathogen can

467
00:29:29,290 --> 00:29:31,470
produce an abscess.
For example,

468
00:29:31,670 --> 00:29:35,010
anaerobic organs normally
found in oral cavities

469
00:29:35,010 --> 00:29:39,470
such as Bacteroides,
Fusobacterium, and Pectococcus.

470
00:29:39,820 --> 00:29:43,450
In some patients,
frequently there is an

471
00:29:43,450 --> 00:29:45,610
antecedent primary
lung infection with

472
00:29:45,610 --> 00:29:49,050
Staphylococcus aureus,
Klebsiella, and

473
00:29:49,050 --> 00:29:52,070
Pneumococcus in a
significant number of cases.

474
00:29:52,890 --> 00:29:59,270
A lung abscess may result
from infected emboli which

475
00:29:59,270 --> 00:30:03,110
may arise in the setting of
thrombophlebitis or from a

476
00:30:03,110 --> 00:30:07,350
friable heart vegetation
in patients with infective

477
00:30:07,350 --> 00:30:11,210
bacterial endocarditis on
the right side of the heart.

478
00:30:11,530 --> 00:30:16,150
The lung abscess formation
is also associated

479
00:30:16,150 --> 00:30:18,810
with traumatic
penetrations of the

480
00:30:18,810 --> 00:30:22,230
lung parenchyma, direct
extension of suppurative

481
00:30:22,230 --> 00:30:25,910
infections of the
esophagus, spine,

482
00:30:26,470 --> 00:30:29,110
subphrenic space
or pleural cavity.

483
00:30:30,390 --> 00:30:31,510
Anyway,

484
00:30:31,630 --> 00:30:34,970
clinical features in
lung abscesses include

485
00:30:35,190 --> 00:30:39,070
a cough with
significant amounts of

486
00:30:39,070 --> 00:30:41,930
full-smelling or
purulent sputum and fever

487
00:30:42,490 --> 00:30:45,910
Also, some patients might
present with chest pain

488
00:30:45,910 --> 00:30:50,030
and weight loss On physical
examination, you might

489
00:30:50,030 --> 00:30:53,910
note in some patients
clubbing of fingers and toes

490
00:30:54,810 --> 00:30:57,530
Anyway, this diagnosis must

491
00:30:57,530 --> 00:30:59,390
be confirmed radiologically

492
00:31:00,250 --> 00:31:03,430
Please note that in
elderly patients,

493
00:31:03,430 --> 00:31:05,950
an associated carcinoma
must always be

494
00:31:05,950 --> 00:31:10,830
ruled out In 10-15%
of elderly patients,

495
00:31:11,130 --> 00:31:12,950
a malignancy is identified

496
00:31:12,950 --> 00:31:15,170
after subsequent investigations

497
00:31:15,470 --> 00:31:16,790
However,

498
00:31:17,630 --> 00:31:18,910
in most patients,

499
00:31:19,130 --> 00:31:21,350
antibiotics are very effective

500
00:31:22,070 --> 00:31:26,470
how unfortunately some
patients would still develop

501
00:31:26,470 --> 00:31:30,770
complications such as pleural
extensions and effusions,

502
00:31:31,290 --> 00:31:32,230
hemorrhage,

503
00:31:32,610 --> 00:31:35,570
secondary brain
abscesses and meningitis,

504
00:31:35,710 --> 00:31:39,350
and rarely secondary
amyloidosis.

505
00:31:41,550 --> 00:31:43,770
Lung abscess morphology.

